fpcmoh: fix use-after-free when the init handshake fails (crashes fprintd with SIGSEGV)

fpi_ssm_mark_failed() completes the state machine synchronously: it runs the
completion callback and then fpi_ssm_free() frees the SSM (fpi-ssm.c,
fpi_ssm_mark_completed). Two init transfer callbacks fall through to
fpi_ssm_next_state() after marking failure, operating on freed memory:

    fpi_ssm_hello_receive_cb: enter
    fpc_hello_evt_handler: cmd id error 6
    BUG: (machine->completed): fpi_ssm_next_state() ../libfprint/fpi-ssm.c:456
    fprintd[...]: segfault at 7fdec9931884 ... in libfprint-2.so.2.0.0

    #0 FP_IS_DEVICE
    #1 __ssm_call_handler
    #2 fpi_ssm_hello_receive_cb
    #3 transfer_finish_cb

Observed three times on FPC 10a5:a900 (Honor MagicBook Art 14, FW 22.26.2.43),
each time right after resume: the sensor answers the first TLS-hello bulk read
with a stale event (cmdid 6 instead of 5) left over from the session that
suspend interrupted. fpc_hello_evt_handler() returns -1, the SSM is freed, and
the fall-through next_state() dereferences it. fpc_cmd_init_cb() has the same
missing return on its failure path.

Return immediately after fpi_ssm_mark_failed() in both callbacks. The open
then fails cleanly through fpc_init_ssm_done() and fprintd's caller can retry;
the retry's USB reset clears the sensor's stale event.

Also drop the error override in fpc_init_ssm_done(): it adopts the SSM's own
error pointer and hands it to the (transfer full) fpi_device_open_complete(),
so the original is freed twice -- once via the GTask, once by fpi_ssm_free().
This is the same bug patch 0004 fixed in fpc_enroll_ssm_done() and
fpc_verify_ssm_done(); the init completion handler was missed. It fires on the
very same failure path, corrupting the heap right before the use-after-free
above.

Not a900-specific: any FPC MoH device whose init handshake fails takes fprintd
down with it.

Applies on top of merge request 396 + patches 0001-0010.
---
--- a/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
@@ -1939,6 +1939,7 @@
     {
       fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm,
                            fpi_device_error_new (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_GENERAL));
+      return;
     }
 
   fpi_ssm_next_state (transfer->ssm);
@@ -1991,8 +1992,11 @@
   int ret = fpc_init_evt_handler (device, transfer->buffer, transfer->actual_length);
 
   if (ret)
-    fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm,
-                         fpi_device_error_new (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_GENERAL));
+    {
+      fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm,
+                           fpi_device_error_new (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_GENERAL));
+      return;
+    }
 
   fpi_ssm_next_state (transfer->ssm);
 }
@@ -2131,8 +2135,11 @@
 {
   FpiDeviceFpcMoh *self = FPI_DEVICE_FPCMOH (dev);
 
-  if (fpi_ssm_get_error (ssm))
-    error = fpi_ssm_get_error (ssm);
+  /* NB: do not overwrite `error` with fpi_ssm_get_error(). libfprint already
+   * passed us an owned copy of the SSM error; fpi_ssm_get_error() returns the
+   * SSM's original, which fpi_ssm_free() frees. Adopting it here would leak the
+   * copy and then double-free the original via the (transfer full)
+   * fpi_device_open_complete(). */
 
   fpi_device_open_complete (dev, error);
   self->task_ssm = NULL;
